<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esagila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Esagila was the great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the most important sanctuary of the city. The rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, lavishly restored it, together with the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a>, as part of their rebuilding of Babylon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esagila.jpg" alt="Esagila" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koldewey, Robert, 1855-1925; Johns, A. S. (Agnes Sophia), 1859-1949, tr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstruction_of_the_peribolos_at_Babylon,_including_the_temple_of_Esagila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Esagila, also spelled Esangil, took its Sumerian name, É-SAǦ-ÍL.LA, to mean temple whose top is lofty. It was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, the protector god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and lay south of the ziggurat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a>. Esarhaddon, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> from 681 to 669 BCE, reconstructed it, claiming to have built it from the foundation to the battlements, a claim corroborated by dedicatory inscriptions found on the stones of the temple's walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple held the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, surrounded by cult images of the cities that had fallen under the hegemony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from the 18th century BCE. It also contained a small lake called the Abzu by the Babylonian priests, a representation of Marduk's father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a>, the god of the waters, who lived in the Abzu, the source of all fresh waters.</p><p>The complex, completed in its final form by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> (604–562 BCE) around earlier cores, was the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. It had a large court of about 40 by 70 metres, containing a smaller court of about 25 by 40 metres, and then the central shrine, made of an anteroom and the inner sanctum that held the statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and his consort Sarpanit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had a statue removed from the Esagila when he flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 482 BCE, desecrated the temple and sacked the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> ordered restorations, and the temple was maintained through the 2nd century BCE as one of the last strongholds of Babylonian culture, including literacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. As Babylon was gradually abandoned under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> the temple decayed in the 1st century BCE.</p><p>Robert Koldewey rediscovered it under a heap of debris in November 1900, and serious examination began in 1910. The rising water table has obliterated much of the sun-dried brick and other oldest material. The Esagila tablet, copied from older texts in 229 BCE, describes the temple in its first fifteen lines before turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a>, and has aided reconstruction. George Smith described the tablet in 1872.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esagila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nebuchadnezzar upheld the ancient Babylonian tradition of royal legitimacy through religious duty, performing the New Year (Akitu) festival rites that affirmed his relationship with the god Marduk and his temple, the <b>Esagila</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
